theory.intro:
For an angle of inclination $\theta$, the trigonometric ratios are defined by a point $P(x,y)$ on a circle of radius $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ centred at the origin: $\cos\theta=\dfrac{x}{r}$, $\sin\theta=\dfrac{y}{r}$, and $\tan\theta=\dfrac{y}{x}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-2.2,0) -- (2.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-2.2) -- (0,2.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (0,0) circle (1.8);
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (1.2,1.34);
\coordinate (A) at (2,0);
\coordinate (X) at (1.2,0);
\draw[thick] (O) -- (P) node[midway, above left] {$r$};
\draw[dashed] (P) -- (X);
\fill (P) circle (1.5pt) node[above right] {$P(x,y)$};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.6cm, angle eccentricity=1.8] {angle = A--O--P};
\node[below left] at (O) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

